  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nA novel approach combining Fourier analysis with stochastic optimization is proposed. Unlike the standard simulated annealing, this method leverages the underlying structure of the problem by starting with a Fourier-informed initial guess. The key innovation is the bidirectional adaptive process where the optimization first searches globally around the initial Fourier guess and then refines locally using gradient descent-style moves. This prevents premature local minima trapping.\n\nThe primary bottleneck addressed is the instability in both the initial guess and the global optimization phase. We replace the fixed cooling schedule with an adaptive temperature decay that depends on neighborhood density and neighborhood types (gradient-like vs energy landscape variations). The repair mechanism focuses on maintaining both box constraints and integral constraints simultaneously through a normalized repair function.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe optimization targets: \nC_5 = max\u2096 \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nsubject to:\n    (i) 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x\n    (ii) \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1\n\nThe representation uses a half-vector h \u2208 \u211d\u207f\u02b0 of length 140 (for n_points=280), with the full function defined by h and its reflection. The gradient is approximated via finite differences with adaptive step sizes:\n\n\u2207J(h) = [ (J(h+\u03f5\u00b7e\u1d62) - J(h)) / (2\u03f5) ] for h optimal moves\n\u2207C(h) = [ (C(h+\u03f5\u00b7e\u1d62) - C(h)) / (2\u03f5) ] for constraint moves\n\nThe constraints are enforced via:\n    h\u1d62 = clip(h\u1d62, 0, 1)\n    \u2211h\u1d62 * \u03c1_weight + \u03bb||h|| regularization term = (n_points/4)/dx \n\nwhere \u03c1_weight is the Lagrange multiplier obtained by cyclic Douglas-Rachford projection.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- Step 0: Initialization\n  - If 'initial_h_values' exists, use it (a 140-element array)\n  - Else, construct a Fourier-informed initial guess \n        h\u2080 = 0.5 * (1 + sin(2\u03c0 * i/period))   with adaptive period\n- Step 1: Global Exploration\n  - For each iteration (1000 episodes):\n      ! Temporal Difference: Focus on periods where historical bests occur\n      ! Gaussian sampling guided by Fourier components\n      ! Repair function:\n          h_clip = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n          if np.sum(h_clip) near n_points/4:\n              return h_clip\n          else:\n              scale = (n_points/4) / np.sum(h_clip)\n              return h_clip * scale\n- Step 2: Local Refinement\n  - Gradient estimation with adaptive step sizing\n  - Projection to feasible set\n  - Dynamic schedule based on function landscape observation:\n        Global phase: Use Fisher information as initial landscape indicator\n        Local phase: Project gradient based on observed Hessian structure\n- Step 3: Adaptive Strategy Switch\n  - Toggle between global and local methods based on:\n        Neighborhood mapping density and gradient consistency\n        Let \u03c6(h) = \u2211\u1d62 |\u2207J(h+e\u1d62) - \u2207(h)| / (1 + ||\u2207(h)||+1)\n        Switch if \u03c6(h) > \u03b8_global_threshold\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nDefault strategy parameters:\n    - Fourier period init: auto-adapted (between 20 and 70)\n    - Exploration vs Exploitation ratio: 0.7:0.3\n    - Mode switching threshold \u03b8 = 0.6\n    - Gradient step size \u03b2: 0.02-0.1\n    - Repair factor \u03b1: 0.9 (80% scaling)\n\nFive core variations tested:\n    1. Gradient Boosted Mode Switching (higher \u03b8)\n    2. Lagrangian Constraint Handling (additional penalty term)\n    3. Neighborhood Density Tracking (adaptation based on local landscape)\n    4. Combined Fourier-Boundary Layer (more aggressive initial guess)\n    5. Cyclic Balancing (oscillating between global and local phases)\n\nSelection trigger:\n    For phase switching, use relative improvement: \n        \u0394_rel = (J(h_new) - J_bound) / |J_prev - J_bound|\n    Switch to local if \u0394_rel > 0.95 when in global phase.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nDominant cost: 280-point correlation (O(n\u00b2) = 78400 operations)\n- Initial phase: 250 iterations\n- Adaptive cooling: T(t) = T0 * (0.95)^(t/100)\n- Budget tracking:\n    elapsed = time.time() - start_time\n    iterations_done = min(int((elapsed/60)*budget_s), max_iter)\n\nFallback: if stuck, escape using an extensionally diverse move:\n    \n    h_rand = np.random.rand(*h.shape)\n    h_repaired = repair(np.clip(h_rand, 0, 1), ...)\n    h_candidate = 0.5 * h + 0.5 * h_repaired\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Feasibility checks:\n    assert 0 <= h_min <= 1 for all h\n    assert np.abs(1 - dx * np.sum(h_full)) < 1e-6\n- Objective validation:\n    h_full = np.concatenate([h, h[::-]])\n    orig_corr = np.max( np.correlate(h_full, 1-h_full)\n    new_corr = new.compute()\n    assert np.abs(orig_corr - new_corr) < 5e-6\n    \n- Return:\n    (full_vector, best_c5, n_points)\n    \n    full_vector = np.concatenate([best_h_opt, best_h_opt[::-1]])",
